I am thinking...: Now I see a critical issue in `asn1_check_tlen`:

```c
if (i & 1)
    plen = len - (p - q);
```

When the length is indefinite (bit 0 of `i` is set), `plen` is set to `len - (p - q)`, which is the remaining data length. This is used in `asn1_find_end`:

```c
if (!asn1_check_tlen(&plen, NULL, NULL, &inf, NULL, &p, len, -1, 0, 0, NULL)) {
    return 0;
}
if (inf)
    expected_eoc++;
else
    p += plen;
len -= p - q;
```

When `inf` is true (indefinite length), `p` is NOT advanced by `plen`. Instead, `expected_eoc` is incremented. The next iteration continues from the same `p`.

But wait - `p` was already advanced by `asn1_check_tlen` to point past the header. So the next iteration will parse the next element inside the indefinite content.

Now, the issue: when `inf` is true, `plen = len - (p - q)`. This is the remaining data from `p` to the end. But `p` has been advanced past the header. So `plen` is the remaining data after the header.

Then `len -= p - q` subtracts the header length from `len`. So `len` now equals `plen` (the remaining data).

The next iteration: `q = p`, and we parse the next element. If it's also indefinite, `plen = len - (p - q) = len - 0 = len`. So `plen` is the remaining data. `expected_eoc++`. `len -= 0` (since `p == q`). So `len` stays the same.

Wait, that can't be right. Let me re-read.

After `asn1_check_tlen`, `p` has been advanced past the header. So `p > q`. Then `len -= p - q` subtracts the header length. So `len` is reduced.

For the next iteration, `q = p` (the new `p`), and we parse the next element. If it's indefinite, `plen = len - (p - q) = len - 0 = len`. So `plen` is the remaining data. `expected_eoc++`. `len -= 0` (since `p == q` after the update). So `len` stays the same.

But wait, `p` was updated by `asn1_check_tlen` to point past the header. So `p > q` after the call. Then `len -= p - q` subtracts the header length. So `len` is reduced.

Hmm, actually I think the issue is different. Let me look at the `asn1_find_end` function again:

```c
while (len > 0) {
    if (asn1_check_eoc(&p, len)) {
        expected_eoc--;
        if (expected_eoc == 0)
            break;
        len -= 2;
        continue;
    }
    q = p;
    if (!asn1_check_tlen(&plen, NULL, NULL, &inf, NULL, &p, len, -1, 0, 0, NULL)) {
        return 0;
    }
    if (inf)
        expected_eoc++;
    else
        p += plen;
    len -= p - q;
}
```

When `inf` is true:
- `p` is advanced past the header by `asn1_check_tlen`
- `expected_eoc++`
- `len -= p - q` (subtracts the header length)

When `inf` is false:
- `p` is advanced past the header by `asn1_check_tlen`
- `p += plen` (advances past the content)
- `len -= p - q` (subtracts the header + content length)

This seems correct. The loop processes each element, advancing `p` and reducing `len` accordingly.

But wait, there's a subtle issue with the `asn1_check_tlen` caching. When `ctx` is not NULL (which it is NOT in `asn1_find_end` - it's passed as NULL), the function caches the parsed header. But in `asn1_find_end`, `ctx` is NULL, so no caching occurs. That's fine.

OK, let me think about this from yet another angle. Maybe the vulnerability is in the `asn1_collect` function:

```c
static int asn1_collect(BUF_MEM *buf, const unsigned char **in, long len,
                        char inf, int tag, int aclass, int depth)
{
    const unsigned char *p;
    ...
    if (cst) {
        if (depth >= ASN1_MAX_STRING_NEST)
            return 0;
        if (!asn1_collect(buf, &p, plen, ininf, tag, aclass, depth + 1))
            return 0;
    } else if (plen && !collect_data(buf, &p, plen))
        return 0;
    ...
}
```

The depth limit is `ASN1_MAX_STRING_NEST = 5`. But what if we have a constructed string with indefinite length that contains many levels of nesting? The depth limit should prevent stack overflow.

But wait - what about the `asn1_find_end` function? It's called from `asn1_d2i_ex_primitive` for SEQUENCE/SET/OTHER types with indefinite length. It doesn't have a depth limit! But it's not recursive, it's iterative (while loop).

OK, I think I need to look at this from a completely different perspective. Let me look at what specific changes were made to fix OSS-Fuzz issue 10129 in the BoringSSL repository.

Actually, let me try a different approach. Let me look at the `asn1_find_end` function more carefully for the case where `inf` is true and the inner element has a definite length with a very large `plen`:

```c
if (inf)
    expected_eoc++;
else
    p += plen;
```

When `inf` is false, `p += plen`. If `plen` is very large (close to LONG_MAX), `p += plen` could overflow/wrap around. But `plen` is checked in `asn1_check_tlen`:
```c
if (!(i & 0x81) && ((plen + ctx->hdrlen) > len)) {
    ...
}
```

This check ensures that `plen + hdrlen <= len`. Since `len` is bounded by `INT_MAX/2` (from `asn1_item_ex_d2i`), `plen` is also bounded. So `p += plen` should not overflow.

But wait, this check is only done when `ctx` is not NULL! In `asn1_find_end`, `ctx` is NULL. So the check is NOT performed!

Let me verify:
```c
if (ctx) {
    ctx->ret = i;
    ctx->plen = plen;
    ctx->pclass = pclass;
    ctx->ptag = ptag;
    ctx->hdrlen = p - q;
    ctx->valid = 1;
    if (!(i & 0x81) && ((plen + ctx->hdrlen) > len)) {
        OPENSSL_PUT_ERROR(ASN1, ASN1_R_TOO_LONG);
        asn1_tlc_clear(ctx);
        return 0;
    }
}
```

Yes! The length check is only inside `if (ctx)`. When `ctx` is NULL (as in `asn1_find_end`), the check is skipped!

So in `asn1_find_end`, if we have an indefinite-length constructed element that contains a definite-length element with a very large length, `plen` could be very large, and `p += plen` could overflow!

But wait, `len` is bounded by `INT_MAX/2` from `asn1_item_ex_d2i`. And `asn1_get_object` checks:
```c
if (*plength > (omax - (p - *pp))) {
    OPENSSL_PUT_ERROR(ASN1, ASN1_R_TOO_LONG);
    ret |= 0x80;
}
```

So if the length is too large, `ASN1_get_object` sets the error bit. Then `asn1_check_tlen` checks:
```c
if (i & 0x80) {
    OPENSSL_PUT_ERROR(ASN1, ASN1_R_BAD_OBJECT_HEADER);
    asn1_tlc_clear(ctx);
    return 0;
}
```

So even without the ctx check, the error from `ASN1_get_object` would be caught. The `ASN1_R_TOO_LONG` error sets bit 0x80 in the return value, which is caught by the `i & 0x80` check.

Hmm, but `ASN1_R_TOO_LONG` sets `ret |= 0x80`, not `i |= 0x80`. Wait, `i` IS the return value of `ASN1_get_object`. So `i` would have bit 0x80 set. And the check `if (i & 0x80)` would catch it.

OK so that path is safe too. Let me think about what else could go wrong.

Actually, let me look at the `asn1_get_length` function more carefully:

```c
static int asn1_get_length(const unsigned char **pp, int *inf, long *rl,
                           long max)
{
    const unsigned char *p = *pp;
    unsigned long ret = 0;
    unsigned long i;

    if (max-- < 1)
        return 0;
    if (*p == 0x80) {
        *inf = 1;
        ret = 0;
        p++;
    } else {
        *inf = 0;
        i = *p & 0x7f;
        if (*(p++) & 0x80) {
            if (i > sizeof(ret) || max < (long)i)
                return 0;
            while (i-- > 0) {
                ret <<= 8L;
                ret |= *(p++);
            }
        } else
            ret = i;
    }
    if (ret > LONG_MAX)
        return 0;
    *pp = p;
    *rl = (long)ret;
    return 1;
}
```

The check `if (ret > LONG_MAX)` ensures that the length fits in a `long`. But `ret` is `unsigned long`, and `LONG_MAX` is `0x7fffffffffffffffL` on 64-bit. So lengths up to `LONG_MAX` are accepted.

Then in `ASN1_get_object`:
```c
if (*plength > (omax - (p - *pp))) {
    OPENSSL_PUT_ERROR(ASN1, ASN1_R_TOO_LONG);
    ret |= 0x80;
}
```

This checks if the length exceeds the available data. But `omax` is `len` which is bounded by `INT_MAX/2`. So `*plength` should be <= `INT_MAX/2`.

